# Shado Cloud — Replica Failover Feature (Design & Decisions)

Status: DESIGN ONLY (not yet implemented). Free-services constraint.

## Goal
If the replica cannot reach the master, the replica takes over and the public
cloudflared-tunnel hostname flips to point at the replica. On takeover the replica
becomes a **read-only master** (serves reads only, rejects writes) to avoid data
divergence. When the original master returns, roles reconcile automatically.

## Core principle
Do NOT let a node self-declare master. An external arbiter decides, and each node
DERIVES its role from "who does the public hostname currently point to." The
cloudflared tunnel / DNS record is that arbiter — only one origin can be the live
target of the hostname at a time. Role becomes dynamic (stored in Redis); the
static `this-service.replication.role` config is only the initial preference.

## Chosen approach: FREE, app-driven (no paid Cloudflare Load Balancer)
Everything used is free: Cloudflare API, DNS, and cloudflared tunnels.
(Rejected: Cloudflare Load Balancer — it is the cleanest independent arbiter, but
it is a paid add-on. User requires free services only, self-hosted.)

### The flip mechanism — two tunnels + DNS repoint
- Each machine (master, replica) runs its OWN named cloudflared tunnel (own UUID).
- Public hostname `cloud.<domain>` is a proxied CNAME → `<master-uuid>.cfargotunnel.com`.
- Failover = update that single DNS record to `<replica-uuid>.cfargotunnel.com` via
  the free Cloudflare API. Proxied record → edge switches within seconds (no TTL wait).
- Do NOT use one shared tunnel on both boxes: Cloudflare would serve from both
  connectors when both are up = split-brain. Two tunnels + DNS pointing at exactly
  one origin is the clean free path.

### Free substitute for independent health checks
Without paid LB there is no edge health check. Instead of the replica judging
"is the master down?" purely from its own reachability (split-brain trap), the
replica ALSO asks Cloudflare (free tunnel-status API): "is the master's tunnel
connector currently connected?" Cloudflare tracks connector health from its own
edge, giving a semi-independent signal.

### Role = "does the public DNS point to me?"
Each node periodically checks the current CNAME target (Cloudflare API or DNS
lookup): if it points to my tunnel UUID → I'm master; else → I'm replica. The DNS
record is the lock; writing it (the flip) IS the promotion. Both nodes converge on
exactly one master.

## Read-only failover (KEY DECISION)
When the replica takes over it becomes a **read-only master**: serves reads
(browse, download, stream, cookie/session validation) but REJECTS writes.

Why this is the right call:
- Kills split-brain data corruption: only one node ever writes, so worst case is
  "stale reads," never divergent/corrupted data — even if the old master is alive
  but partitioned.
- Makes failback trivial: the promoted replica never diverged, so when the master
  returns there is nothing to reconcile/merge. Master stays source of truth; replica
  reverts to background sync; DNS flips home.
- Reframes the promoted node accurately: it is a read-replica temporarily exposed to
  the public, not a true new master (simpler, less code).
- Lets us be MORE relaxed about false-positive failovers: a wrong promotion only
  causes a few minutes of read-only, not corruption — lowers the bar for the free arbiter.

### "What is a write?" — must gate these
Block user-facing writes AND make incidental/telemetry writes skip silently:
- File mutations: upload, delete, rename, move, share / temp-URL creation.
- Temp-URL request counters, file access stats, last-accessed timestamps.
- `LoggerToDb` — must fall back to console logging (DB logging would fail).
- Metrics pusher / heartbeat / replica registry writes.
- Auth: cookie VALIDATION is a read (existing sessions keep working); LOGIN/REGISTER
  are writes and will be down during failover (accepted degraded mode).

### Enforcement layers
- Primary: app-level read-only mode derived from role. Global guard rejects mutating
  ops (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE + mutating GETs) with a clear 503 "read-only failover
  mode" so the frontend can show a banner. Incidental/telemetry writes skip silently.
- Defense-in-depth (optional, later): DB-level read-only (SELECT-only MySQL user or
  read-only sessions) once all incidental writes are gated.

## Failover sequence (replica → read-only master)
1. Replica coordinator loop, over a sustained window (hysteresis, e.g. several minutes):
   (a) cannot reach master, AND
   (b) Cloudflare reports master's tunnel disconnected, AND
   (c) replica itself has good internet (can reach Cloudflare API + neutral endpoints
       — proves the replica is not the isolated one).
2. Replica calls Cloudflare API to repoint the CNAME to its own tunnel.
3. Replica flips internal role → (read-only) master: STOPS pull crons (so it won't
   overwrite its now-authoritative data from a dead master), sets readOnly = true,
   serves reads.
4. Emails shadosite@gmail.com that failover happened.

## Old master recovery (demotion / failback)
- On boot and periodically, old master checks "does the DNS point to me?" If it lost
  the record → demote to replica, stop acting as master, re-sync FROM the new active
  node. Because role is derived from the DNS lock, this is automatic and split-brain-safe
  (losing side voluntarily steps down).
- Failback policy: MANUAL / sticky. Once the replica is active, keep it there until you
  deliberately flip back during a quiet window (avoids flapping). With read-only failover
  there is no data-loss surprise on failback anyway.

## Fencing (free version's weak spot — must handle)
The tunnel/DNS MUST be the ONLY ingress to either machine. No direct public IP, no
alternate hostname, no LAN clients pointed at the origins — otherwise an alive-but-
partitioned old master could still receive writes. The self-demote check ("do I still
own the DNS record?") is the backstop. (Read-only failover already removes the
corruption risk even if fencing is imperfect.)

## Data consistency / RPO caveat (unchanged)
Replication is ASYNC (files every minute, DB hourly). Failover RPO ≈ up to ~1 hour of
DB writes / ~1 minute of file writes may be lost (writes accepted after the last
successful replication and before failure). Read-only failover avoids ADDITIONAL
divergence but does not recover this pre-existing async gap. To shrink it later:
more frequent DB sync, or continuous replication (MySQL binlog/GTID) — larger change.

## What to build (all free)
- Cloudflare API token (scoped: edit the DNS zone + read tunnel status), stored in config.
- Coordinator loop on each node: check DNS ownership + master tunnel status; apply
  promote/demote; start/stop the pull crons; set/clear read-only mode.
- Dynamic role in Redis (static config = initial preference only).
- Replica-side guards: connectivity sanity check + hysteresis.
- Read-only mode flag + global mutating-request guard (503 + frontend banner);
  gate/skip incidental writes; LoggerToDb console fallback.
- `/health` endpoint (useful for own monitoring even without LB).
- Failover email alerts (reuse EmailService) + documented MANUAL failback procedure.

## Optional free hardening
Add an independent external witness the replica must also consult before promoting:
free-tier uptime monitor (e.g. UptimeRobot), GitHub Actions cron, or a free-tier
serverless function hitting the master. Reduces false-positive promotions further.

## Open decisions to confirm before implementing
1. Confirm degraded UX acceptable: during failover users can browse/download/stream but
   cannot upload/delete/share, and cannot create NEW logins (existing sessions keep working).
2. Exact read/write line: default = all GET reads allowed (except mutating ones),
   everything else blocked, telemetry writes silently skipped.
3. Frontend read-only banner when it receives the failover 503?
4. RPO tolerance: is "up to ~1 hour of DB loss" acceptable, or do we need more frequent
   DB sync / binlog replication first?
5. Auto vs manual failback (recommended: manual/sticky).
6. Is the tunnel the ONLY ingress, or are there direct/LAN paths to fence off?

